---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import HeroCover from "../../components/HeroCover.astro";
import TagList from "../../components/TagList.astro";
import MetaBox from "../../components/MetaBox.astro";
import MetaItem from "../../components/MetaItem.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Media from "../../components/Media.astro";
import TransitionCallout from "../../components/TransitionCallout.astro";
import CalloutList from "../../components/CalloutList.astro";
import CalloutItem from "../../components/CalloutItem.astro";
import Confront from "../../components/Confront.astro";
import Spacer from "../../components/Spacer.astro";

const title =
  "I turned fragmented Design System changes into a shared, searchable source of truth";

const heroTags = [
  { label: "Shipped", dot: "success" as const },
  { label: "Design System" },
  { label: "UX" },
  { label: "UI" },
];

const heroMeta = [
  { label: "ROLE", value: "Full end-to-end ownership" },
  { label: "TIMELINE", value: "6 weeks" },
  {
    label: "TECHNIQUES",
    value:
      "Data Analysis, Benchmark, Interviews, Flow Mapping, Prototyping, User Tests",
  },
  { label: "TOOLS", value: "Claude Code, Figjam" },
];

const cover = "/playground/placeholder.svg";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title={title}>
  <HeroCover src={cover} alt="Case study cover" />

  <article class="case-study">
    <Spacer size="md" />

    <section class="hero-content">
      <h1 class="type-display" style="color: var(--color-text-heading);">
        {title}
      </h1>
      <TagList tags={heroTags} />
    </section>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <MetaBox>
      {heroMeta.map((m) => <MetaItem label={m.label} value={m.value} />)}
    </MetaBox>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- BACKGROUND -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="The background"
        title="One complex system growing at different speed"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        I'm part of a <strong>3-person Design System team</strong>, still
        fairly new. We maintain <strong>4 core libraries</strong>; UI
        designers use them across different products. The governance system
        was slowly built over the past years. There's still a lot to figure
        out, and I joined while it was taking shape.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        Every project runs on its own timeline, so at any given moment,
        different designers can be working with{" "}
        <strong>different versions of the same component, all at once</strong>.
        That much movement naturally creates entropy. Our job is to fight it:{" "}
        <strong>rationalize, simplify, keep things fast</strong>.
      </p>
      <Media
        src="/case-studies/design-system-search/diagram-teams.jpg"
        alt="Applications and product teams responsibilities diagram"
        caption="TEAMS' RESPONSABILITIES AND INTERACTION"
      />
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- PROBLEM -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Problem"
        title="The design system team was turning into a help desk"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        Even though we had a process to communicate updates, the{" "}
        <strong>same questions kept coming back</strong>:
      </p>
      <Media
        src="/case-studies/design-system-search/diagram-questions.jpg"
        alt="Recurring questions from designers"
      />
      <MetaBox variant="data">
        <MetaItem label="TICKET ANALYSIS - 1 YEAR PERIOD">
          <strong>12%</strong> of <strong>273</strong> requests where about <strong>component changes</strong> or which version to use
        </MetaItem>
      </MetaBox>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <TransitionCallout
      title="This impacted our work in two ways"
      variant="warning"
    />

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <!-- PAIN POINTS -->
    <section class="section-tight">
      <p class="eyebrow-line">PAIN POINTS</p>
      <CalloutList>
        <CalloutItem
          title="Hours lost answering questions"
          variant="warning"
          iconStyle="filled"
        >
          <svg
            slot="icon"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          >
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle>
            <polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"></polyline>
          </svg>
          In the last year, our team spent up to a day for each question to
          recover the info on changes trough slack, email, and asking around
        </CalloutItem>
        <CalloutItem
          title="Risk of using wrong components"
          variant="warning"
          iconStyle="outlined"
        >
          <svg
            slot="icon"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          >
            <path
              d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"
            ></path>
            <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"></line>
            <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"></line>
          </svg>
          Designers struggled to pick the right version for each project,
          risking outdated components in their flows, and having to replace
          them later
        </CalloutItem>
      </CalloutList>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <p class="body-copy">
      We already had a process to communicate changes but{" "}
      <strong>it wasn't working</strong>. I needed to find out why.
    </p>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- DISCOVERY -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Discovery"
        title="There was no visibility into changes on the libraries"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        To understand why, I <strong>audited the workflows</strong>, and{" "}
        <strong>interviewed everyone</strong> that worked with the design
        system.
      </p>
      <Media
        src={cover}
        alt="Actual workflow between UX/UI and Design System teams"
        caption="THE ACTUAL WORKFLOW AND THE MAJOR PAINT POINT"
      />
    </section>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <MetaBox variant="insight">
      <MetaItem
        label="INSIGHT"
        value="Changes were communicated, but there was no shared source of truth for tracking and retrieving them."
      />
    </MetaBox>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <TransitionCallout
      title="I defined project objectives to guide the design phase"
      variant="info"
    />

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <!-- OUR NEEDS -->
    <section class="section-tight">
      <p class="eyebrow-line">OUR NEEDS</p>
      <CalloutList>
        <CalloutItem
          title="Retrievabile"
          variant="info"
          iconStyle="filled"
        >
          <svg
            slot="icon"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          >
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
            <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
          </svg>
          Changes need to live somewhere stable and be easy to find later
        </CalloutItem>
        <CalloutItem
          title="Frictionless"
          variant="info"
          iconStyle="outlined"
        >
          <svg
            slot="icon"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          >
            <path d="M13 2 L3 14 h9 l-1 8 10-12 h-9 z"></path>
          </svg>
          The solution needs to fit into UX/UI workflow as they will be using
          it in their UI Kits
        </CalloutItem>
        <CalloutItem
          title="Proactive"
          variant="info"
          iconStyle="filled"
        >
          <svg
            slot="icon"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
          >
            <path
              d="M18 8A6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 7-3 9-3 9h18s-3-2-3-9"
            ></path>
            <path d="M13.73 21a2 2 0 0 1-3.46 0"></path>
          </svg>
          Changes need to reach people, not just wait to be found
        </CalloutItem>
      </CalloutList>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="sm" />

    <MetaBox variant="hmw">
      <MetaItem
        label="HOW MIGHT WE"
        value="Make component changes easy to track and retrieve?"
      />
    </MetaBox>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- EVALUATION / RESEARCH & CONSTRAINTS -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Research & Constraints"
        title="A changelog could close the information gap, but no viable solution satisfied our needs and workflow"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        I <strong>benchmarked 10 Design Systems</strong> to understand how
        other teams handled component evolution.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>8 used changelogs or release notes</strong> as part of their
        documentation, which confirmed that a changelog was a sensible
        direction.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        I then <strong>evaluated the available options</strong> against the
        teams' needs and feedback:
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
        <em>Card grid comparing the four options — awaiting <code>Card</code> component.</em>
      </p>
      <MetaBox variant="decision">
        <MetaItem
          label="DESIGN DECISION"
          value="No existing option satisfied our needs, so I chose to develop a custom plugin for Figma."
        />
      </MetaBox>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- STRATEGY -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Strategy"
        title="I used AI to iterate faster and surface technical constraints"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        This was a new domain for me, and I was{" "}
        <strong>designing a software system from scratch</strong> without a
        predefined architecture or dedicated engineering resource. I knew the
        objectives, but not yet how all the technical pieces would fit
        together.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        I defined lightweight requirements,{" "}
        <strong>used Claude Code to build functional versions of the plugin</strong>,
        tested them in the real workflow, and refined the architecture as I
        learned. This allowed me to make technical and design decisions{" "}
        <strong>based on real usage rather than assumptions</strong>, while
        making the most of the limited time available.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- PIVOTS -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Pivots"
        title="From a changelog plugin, to an AI-ready infrastructure"
      />
      <p class="body-copy">
        As I <strong>tested the workflow in real files</strong>, each
        iteration exposed something I hadn't accounted for. These findings
        changed where information should live, how much effort designers
        should spend recording it, and how the system should handle the
        growing history.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="md" />

    <div class="pivot-block">
      <h3 class="type-h2" style="color: var(--color-text-heading);">
        01/ Reduce the effort to log a change
      </h3>
      <Confront
        tabs={[
          { label: "AFTER", value: "after" },
          { label: "BEFORE", value: "before" },
        ]}
      >
        <div
          data-panel="after"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
        <div
          data-panel="before"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
      </Confront>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Initial approach:</strong> I didn't know the technology so I
        started simple by adding tables under each component for designers to
        fill in, then the plugin would collect the changes into a list ready
        to be shared on Slack.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Evidence:</strong> Designers filled in the minimum details
        possible. We weren't getting the information we needed and the
        workflow was too demanding.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Pivot:</strong> I introduced a guided form that captured what
        we actually needed, and automated anything that could be automated,
        like creating the table for new components.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Spacer size="md" />

    <div class="pivot-block">
      <h3 class="type-h2" style="color: var(--color-text-heading);">
        02/ Put changes where designers see them
      </h3>
      <Confront
        tabs={[
          { label: "AFTER", value: "after" },
          { label: "BEFORE", value: "before" },
        ]}
      >
        <div
          data-panel="after"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
        <div
          data-panel="before"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
      </Confront>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Evidence:</strong> The changelog on libraries made changes
        visible, but designers wouldn't necessarily consult it as they pulled
        components straight from the assets panel and never saw it in other
        files.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Pivot:</strong> Version info is now written automatically
        into the component's description, so it's visible to everyone. A
        warning icon on component's titles help designers notice an evolution
        at a glance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Spacer size="md" />

    <div class="pivot-block">
      <h3 class="type-h2" style="color: var(--color-text-heading);">
        03/ Create a reliable source of truth
      </h3>
      <Confront
        tabs={[
          { label: "AFTER", value: "after" },
          { label: "BEFORE", value: "before" },
        ]}
      >
        <div
          data-panel="after"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
        <div
          data-panel="before"
          class="pivot-panel"
          style="background-image: url('/playground/placeholder.svg');"
        >
        </div>
      </Confront>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Initial approach:</strong> All component changes were stored
        in Figma before being sent to Slack for review.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Evidence:</strong> Storing the data directly in Figma made it
        easy to overwrite changes when multiple designers edited the same
        file, risking data loss and duplicated entries. It was also difficult
        to track which changes had already been shared on Slack.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        <strong>Pivot:</strong> I synced the changes to a GitHub repository,
        giving us a more reliable and structured way to store the data and
        making it usable by AI. To help designers understand what was still
        waiting to be shared, I introduced a Sync Queue: basically a cart
        where pending changes wait for the next sync.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- SOLUTION -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Solution"
        title="Making information easy to access, and where designers work"
      />
      <Media src={cover} alt="Overview of the plugin experience" />
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- IMPACT -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Impact"
        title="From hours of investigation to a shared source of truth"
      />
      <p class="body-copy" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
        <em>Awaiting <code>ImpactCard</code> / <code>MetricRow</code> / <code>Quote</code> — content:</em>
      </p>
      <ul class="body-copy" style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li>
          <strong>Recover change context</strong> — AFTER ✅ ~5 min · BEFORE up to 1 working day. Quote: "Designers can now retrieve the context behind a past component change without reconstructing it from Slack, email and conversations"
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Release note: everyone up-to-date</strong> — AFTER ✅ ~25 min of review · BEFORE ~4h manual work. Quote: "The weekly release note is generated automatically from the same structured change data and sent to Slack for review."
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reduce the risk of outdated components</strong> — AFTER ✅ Component history and replacement context are available where needed · BEFORE Changes could be missed and outdated versions could reach final files
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <Spacer size="lg" />

    <!-- LEARNINGS -->
    <section class="section">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="Learnings"
        title="What this project changed in the way I work"
      />
      <p class="body-copy" style="color: var(--color-text-muted);">
        <em>Awaiting <code>LearningRow</code> — content:</em>
      </p>
      <div class="body-copy">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">
          <strong>I learned how to do design in a "terminal"</strong>
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 1.5rem 0;">
          The faster I could generate and change working software, the easier it became to lose the rationale behind decisions. I kept a lightweight change and decision log and repeatedly checked new ideas against the original goals to avoid scope drift.
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;">
          <strong>I learned to never trust AI, no matter how confident it sounds</strong>
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          AI suggestions can sound convincing, especially outside your strongest technical domain. I learned to challenge them, ask for alternatives and validate doubts with experts. One example was moving from one large Markdown changelog to a component-centric structure after investigating retrieval and maintainability.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 760px);
    justify-content: center;
    padding-block-end: var(--spacing-gap-section-lg);
    padding-inline: var(--spacing-gutter-page);
  }

  .hero-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-gap-xl);
  }

  .section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-gap-xl);
  }

  .section-tight {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-gap-md);
  }

  .pivot-block {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-gap-xl);
  }

  .body-copy {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-body);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-light);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    font-variation-settings: "opsz" 14;
  }

  .eyebrow-line {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    font-size: var(--text-eyebrow);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
  }

  .pivot-panel {
    min-height: 360px;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
  }
</style>
